# Playtest notes / backlog

Open work only. When an item is done it **moves** to [`doc/dev/history.md`](doc/dev/history.md) — it does not get a checkmark and stay here, which is how this file grew to 33 completed entries and an item complaining about them.

Durable rationale ("why is the code like this?") belongs in [`doc/dev/decisions.md`](doc/dev/decisions.md); the chronological record, including approaches measured and reverted, is in `history.md`.

**Audited 2026-08-10.** Every open item making a checkable claim about the code was verified against `master` that day. Four had been overtaken by fixes and are rewritten: the wedge item (loot treadmill *and* its C#-parser successor both fixed), the level-1 disagreement (grid-equality gate shipped in both harnesses), and walking-distance site (1) (`rankExitBlockers`, on by default). Two of them had already steered a "what next" decision into work that was already done, which is what prompted the sweep.

Confirmed **still true** in the same pass, so they need no re-checking: `pickThreat` gates and ranks on `Math.hypot` and filters `e.alive && e.aggroed` (so Stage 6 and walking-distance site (2) are both real); no threat give-up exists (reverted in `8390c8e`); `media.peerconnection.ice.loopback` is still untried and the Firefox CI skip is still in place; `DECORATIONS_ENABLED` is still false; `goto` teleporters can still pair inside one room; and reloading, die-screams, deeplinks, public highscores and the online-WAD preference are all still absent.

Items that record a *measurement* rather than a code claim (the demo-campaign difficulty numbers, the damage-source breakdowns, the harness-specific wedge finding) were not re-run — they are history, and their dates say when they were true.

## Open

- [ ] **`verify (multiplayer-transition)` hangs once in four runs. RE-FRAMED 2026-08-10: it is not a wedge, and `BOT_NAV_STALL_BAIL_TICKS` is not the guard that was missing.** A deadline plus diagnostics has shipped so the next occurrence is readable; the cause is still open.
  - **The original framing (kept, because it was wrong in an instructive way).** Run `31331886895` sat **19.5 minutes** on `Host: navigating (invulnerable) to the real exit...` and died on the job's 20-minute timeout (`cancelled`, not a failed assertion); the same commit passed at 8m37s and 7m23s either side of it. That was read as "a wedge whose early bail did not fire".
  - **Every budget in this path is counted in *decisions*, and they are all bounded — so nothing here loops forever.** What is unbounded is the *wall clock* they translate to: `driveTowardWithReplan` allows 160 decisions per waypoint x 3 re-plans, and at this harness's real 300-400ms decision window a 40-waypoint route is legally bounded at **over two hours**. Measured against that, a healthy level-1 host drive costs **806 decisions** (`diagnose-level-wedge.mjs --mp-shape --ignore-threats --god-mode --level 1`, 2026-08-10). The 19.5-minute run is roughly **3.6x** the normal decision count: unlucky, but entirely legal.
  - **`BOT_NAV_STALL_BAIL_TICKS` was never going to catch it.** It fires only when the bot stops *moving* — it resets its anchor whenever the player travels past `BOT_NAV_STALL_RADIUS_TILES`. A bot grinding through re-planned waypoints is moving the whole time. "The bail did not fire" was true and irrelevant; do not spend time arming it harder.
  - **What shipped (2026-08-10):** one wall-clock budget (`HOST_NAV_DEADLINE_MS`, 9 min) shared by the route and exit-push phases of `verify-multiplayer-transition.mjs`, failing with `reportAnomalies` + `dumpExitGate` instead of letting the job die silently. A healthy run is unaffected (verified end to end at 7m07s locally). Verified *firing* too, by forcing the budget to 25s via `CODEENSTEIN_TRANSITION_NAV_DEADLINE_MS` — a guard nobody has watched fire is a guard nobody knows works.
  - **A lead, explicitly not a finding.** In that forced-25s sample the activity breakdown read `loot 36.2t (75%), route 12.2t (25%)` — three quarters of the host's movement was loot detours, in a phase whose entire job is to reach the exit. That is the loot-treadmill item's own signature showing up in this harness, and it would explain a 3.6x decision count without any wedge. But it is **one 25-second window early in one run**, which is exactly when loot is densest and the route barely started — it proves nothing on its own. The next real occurrence now dumps the same breakdown across the *whole* phase; compare there before acting.
  - Do not re-diagnose from the job summary: it prints one line per phase, so it cannot distinguish "stuck at a waypoint" from "walking a very long way". `diagnose-level-wedge.mjs --mp-shape` reproduces the decision shape without a session (seconds per attempt), but not the transport — no lockstep input delay, no real `evaluate` latency.

- [ ] **The wedge is HARNESS-SPECIFIC, and an A/B run in the wrong harness produced a confident wrong conclusion (2026-08-09). Read this before touching the give-up again.**
  - `diagnose-level-wedge.mjs:7` already says it: *"the wedge only reproduces under `scripts/generate-default-highscore.mjs`"*, and line 12 records that two days went into diagnosing the wrong harness. The two build `Bot` differently (`recordStepMs`, logger presence). I then A/B'd the threat give-up under `run-balancing-capture.mjs` — the harness that does **not** wedge — saw **0 wedges in both arms**, and concluded the give-up "never prevented wedges". That is a zero from a check whose trigger never fired.
  - **In the harness that does show it, three consecutive observations disagree with that conclusion:**

    | regen | give-up | wedges |
    |---|---|---|
    | 16:33 | absent | L6, L15 |
    | 17:53 | **active** | **none**, and 4.9x faster (Casual 39m50s -> 8m09s) |
    | 20:18 | reverted | L6 x3 |

  - **So the give-up is a real trade, not a no-op.** It suppresses generator-harness wedges *and* it costs half a campaign of survival: demo-campaign hard, 45 runs per arm, level-visits 309 vs 667 and clear rate 85.4% vs 93.4%, Fisher **p = 0.0001**. Reverted (`8390c8e`) because dying earlier is worse than a wedge in one tooling path — but the revert message's claim that it "never did the job it was written for" is **wrong** and is corrected here.
  - **The rule this cost**: before running an A/B, confirm the phenomenon under test actually occurs in the harness you are running it in. Measure the base rate in the control arm *first* — a control that shows zero occurrences cannot demonstrate anything about a fix, however clean the statistics look afterwards.
  - **Still open**: the generator harness wedges at demo-campaign L6 without the give-up, which makes `defaultHighscore.ts` regeneration slow and occasionally cap-bound. A future fix must be validated on **death rate in the capture harness** *and* **wedge rate in the generator harness** — neither alone is sufficient, which is the whole lesson.

- [ ] **demo-campaign measured with a correct bot (2026-08-09): 14 of its 17 levels are free, and L15 is the entire campaign. Measurement only — no content changed.**
  - **Hard, 45 runs, bot behaving correctly** (the give-up A/B's OFF arm, which is what HEAD now does):

    ```
    L1-L9 45/45   L10 44/45   L11 43/44   L12 43/43   L13 43/43   L14 42/43
    L15 1/42      L16 1/1     L17 1/1
    ```

    Three deaths across levels 1-14 combined, then **L15 kills 41 of 42**. L16 and L17 clear whenever they are reached. So the campaign's difficulty is not spread thin — it is one level, and everything after it is a formality.
  - **This is the same shape the old bot showed** (2026-08-05: L1-14 flawless 16/16, L15 8/16, L17 0/8), so it is not an artefact of this bot. What changed is that L17 is now clearable — the Elite cap turned its 3 Elites from ~3,225 HP each into packs of ~500, and total room HP is essentially untouched (9,665 vs 9,675). The wall moved from "L15 *and* L17" to "L15 alone".
  - **The premise of the question holds, with a caveat.** The campaign was tuned against a bot with at least four defects, and `ELITE_HP_MULTIPLIER` was halved 4->2 specifically because the bot died 12/12 on level 12 — a fight it was knife-trading. But *this* measurement does not rest on that: 14 levels at ~99% clear on Hard is the finding, and it reproduces across two very different bots.
  - **What "harder" has to mean now.** Elites are capped, so a single huge function can no longer make a wall; the measured difficulty driver is exposure-time x enemy count (three A/Bs, total damage taken invariant at 122/127/126). Harder levels therefore mean source files with **more callable entities**, not one monster function. And L1-14 being gentle may be deliberate — this is the showcase campaign — so the case is strongest for the back half.
  - **Easy/normal numbers from the same session are CONTAMINATED** and must not be quoted: they come from the full 3-difficulty capture, which ran with the give-up active (reverted in `8390c8e` as a regression). Only the hard arm above is clean. Re-measure easy/normal before using them.
  - **The bot is not a difficulty oracle.** It is superhuman at some things and far worse at others — measured taking up to 53% of its damage while not fighting. "The bot clears it" is evidence the content is unchallenging *to this instrument*, not that a player will breeze it. What makes the case here is the margin (3 deaths in 14 levels x 45 runs), not the bare clear rate.
  - **Sequencing trap**: `defaultHighscore.ts` is generated from this campaign, so any content change invalidates it. Regenerate *after* deciding difficulty, not before — this session regenerated twice for exactly this reason.

- [ ] **The bot is destined to become the deathmatch opponent, which changes what "good enough" means for every open bot item (user, 2026-08-09).** Recorded because it reorders the backlog rather than adding to it.
  - **The bar changes.** As an *instrument* the bot only has to be unbiased and stable — it may play badly as long as it plays badly *consistently*, which is why several defects were tolerable. As an *opponent* it has to be good and fun, and "consistently mediocre" is the failure mode rather than the escape hatch. `multiplayer-research.md` already files "adapt balancing AI for deathmatch/bots" as its own initiative and warns it is not an adaptation of existing code; this is the reason to believe that.
  - **Which open items get promoted.** They stop being measurement noise and become gameplay bugs the player would *see*:
    - **Threats have no give-up** — an opponent that locks onto something it cannot reach and stops playing is unmissable in PvP.
    - **`pickThreat` uses `visible` only as a sort key, never a filter** — an opponent tracking you through a wall reads as cheating, which is worse than reading as stupid.
    - **The wedges** (curl 2.0% of level-visits, improved but unfixed) — a bot that stops moving is the single most visible failure a deathmatch opponent can have.
    - **Route/replan churn**, still unresolved.
  - **Three design choices are instrument-shaped and would be wrong for an opponent.** Worth knowing before anyone treats the current bot as a starting point:
    1. **Keyboard-only input.** `dispatchSegment` sends synthetic `KeyboardEvent`s and deliberately never the mouse. Aim is therefore quantised to key-hold durations — today's turn-split got the *granularity* right, but the mechanism is still "hold a turn key for N ms", not "point here". A credible deathmatch opponent wants direct aim.
    2. **A 50ms decision window** (`VIRTUAL_STEP_MS`), i.e. 20 decisions/sec, and `MultiplayerBot` raises `minDecisionMs` further for lockstep. Fine for measurement, coarse for duelling.
    3. **The profiles are bot-shaped, not player-shaped.** Casual/Gamer/Pro exist to span a *measurement* range (`fireAngleEps`, `ammoThrift`, `selfHarmAversion`); as difficulty tiers a player picks, they would want re-deriving from "how does this feel to fight" rather than "how does this spread telemetry".
  - **Today's aim fix is on this path**, not a detour: `turnSplitIntent` is the first change made because the bot should *play* better rather than because a metric was contaminated.
  - **The related architectural item already exists** in this file — "decouple bot decisions from engine, for more performance ... maybe even thread the bot(s)" — and its "multiple bots" clause is exactly this. Do not scope deathmatch bots without it.

- [ ] **Three notions of "level 1" still exist — but the run-losing half is FIXED (verified 2026-08-10).** The detector shipped; the duplication it detects did not go away.
  - **What is fixed.** `planEngineMatch.mjs` compares the engine's live grid against the planned map's, explicitly *not* enemy count (curl's two candidates both held exactly 2 enemies, which is how the old pre-flight passed while every route was computed for the wrong map). `assertPlanMatchesEngine` runs at level start, before anything drives, in **both** harnesses that spend runs — `run-balancing-telemetry.mjs` and `generate-default-highscore.mjs` — and kills the process on a mismatch. The 58-runs-lost failure cannot recur silently.
  - **The original defect, for context.** `planLevels` enumerates in **filename order** and hands the bot a route for slot 1; the game picks its entry with `findEntrypointByScanning`, which returns `bestWithMain ?? bestOverall ?? firstParsed` — the cheapest file *containing a `main`/`Main`*, falling back to cheapest-overall only when none has one. Measured on curl: planner slot 1 = `01_projects_OS400_make-docs.sh` (complexity 1, no `main`), engine loaded `03_docs_examples_pop3-stat.c` (complexity 4, has `int main(void)`) — a zero-diff match against planned index 2 and **205 differing tiles** against index 0. The bot then walks a route valid for a map it is not standing on and jams against a wall its planner believes is floor; on curl it pinned at `y=31.75`, exactly the tile boundary at 32.0 minus the 0.2 player radius, which is axis-separated collision sliding and not heading oscillation. The anomaly scan labels it `oscillation`, which is why it was misdiagnosed as a navigation bug for most of a day.
  - **What is still open, and it is a cleanup rather than a hazard.** Game, solver and planner each still decide "level 1" independently; the gate *detects* the disagreement instead of removing it. Either `planLevels` adopts the browser's entrypoint rule, or the browser plays in the planner's order. Doing it would make `planEngineMatch` redundant — which is the tell that it is the real fix.
  - **Staging cannot substitute for either.** `stage-campaign.mjs`'s `order()` guard keeps slot 1 at the global complexity minimum, modelling only the `bestOverall` branch, so a campaign whose cheapest file lacks `main` while another has one still diverges. It also filters zero-complexity levels using the *solver's* complexity, which is not the number the browser's parser computes — curl's `make-docs.sh` passed that filter and still scored 1 in the browser (tree-sitter-bash emits top-level `variable_assignment` nodes that `GLOBAL_CONTAINER` matches).
- [ ] **ANSWERED 2026-08-10: `expectedDamagePerShot` is exactly right for single-pellet weapons and ~2.6x optimistic for multi-pellet ones. The open work is now a fix + A/B, not an investigation.** Measured with `npm run report:damage-model` (new) over **1,175,847 ranged shots** across 954 event files already on disk — no new capture.
  - **The decomposition that settled it.** A `shot` event and every `damageDealt` it caused share a timestamp, so a shot's total damage is recoverable and the shots can be split into those that dealt nothing and those that connected. "Model too generous" and "the bot misses" then separate cleanly: `dmg/hit ÷ predicted` tests the model's own claim on shots that *did* land, while `dmg/shot ÷ predicted` additionally carries the misses.
  - **Single-pellet weapons: the model is exact, and the shortfall is aim.** Pistol lands **22.0** damage per connecting shot against 22.0 predicted (ratio **1.00**); gdb **12.0** against 12.0 (**1.00**). Their whole gap to prediction is the **~30% of shots that miss entirely** (pistol 29-35%, gdb 29-31% across 0-8 tiles). That is a bot-accuracy number, not a model number — and it is worth its own item: the bot misses roughly one ranged shot in three.
  - **Multi-pellet weapons: the model is wrong, on shots that hit.** Shotgun at 0-2 tiles connects on **95%** of shots and still delivers only **66.8** damage against **175** predicted — ratio **0.38**, i.e. **2.67 of 7 pellets** land where the model claims all 7. It is not a miss problem; the shot connects and simply does less than claimed. The gap narrows with range only because the model's own prediction falls faster than reality: 0.42 at 2-4t, 0.47 at 4-6t, 0.67 at 6-8t. Friday Hotfix shows the same shape at **0.61-0.64** (3.7 of 6 pellets).
  - **So the defect is `hitFraction`**, the multi-pellet term: `halfWidthPx / scatterPx` clamped to 1, which at close range credits *every* pellet because the sprite is wider than the scatter. The engine's real per-pellet raycast evidently does not agree. The single-pellet path never exercises that term, which is exactly why it is perfect.
  - **Consequence, and why it matters beyond one branch**: the shotgun is systematically over-valued against pistol/gdb everywhere in `pickRangedWeapon`, not just in the cluster branch — which is precisely the effect the old ghidra item mistook for a short-circuit. Correcting `hitFraction` would change weapon selection across the board, so it needs the 3-combo A/B protocol at n=12, and it is a *bot-behaviour* change: do not land it inside another measurement.
  - **The 2.6x is a lower bound.** One blast's damage to a *second* enemy counts toward that shot, which inflates the measured side; the true per-target figure is worse.
  - **ghidra cannot be measured this way and its rows are not evidence.** A rocket has flight time, so its damage lands at a *later* timestamp than the shot and the join cannot see it — hence the nonsensical 100% miss rate on its rows. Any ghidra calibration needs a different join (nearest-later damage within a flight-time window), which is unbuilt.
  - Small-n rows (shotgun 8+, pistol/gdb 12+, Friday past its 6.5-tile max range) show ratios above 1 purely because the model's fog falloff collapses its prediction; ignore them.

- [ ] **ANSWERED 2026-08-11: the ~30% miss rate is TWO defects, and the bigger one is not aim precision — it is that the bot shoots at where the target *was*.** Measured with `npm run report:aim-error` (new) over the same 1.1M shots already on disk; no new capture. Both fixes are bot-behaviour changes, so neither may land inside another measurement.
  - **Defect 1 — the fire gate is dimensionally wrong.** The bot fires once its heading error is inside `profile.fireAngleEps` (0.08/0.05/0.03 rad), a *constant*, while the angle a target subtends falls off as `1/dist`. They cross at ~5.8 tiles for Gamer, so past ~6 tiles the gate permits shots the geometry cannot land. Two independent signatures: normal-enemy hit rate decays 94% → 49% from 0-2t to 12+t, and the pistol/shotgun gap widens from **3pp at 0-2t to 27pp at 6-8t** — the shotgun's 70px spread rescuing exactly the shots the single pellet's angular error loses. Fix: gate on `min(fireAngleEps, angularHalfWidth(dist, arch))`, both terms already in hand at the call site.
  - **Defect 2 — the aim is one decision window stale, and this is the dominant term.** `bot.mjs` samples enemy positions at the end of one pump, `decide()` runs on that snapshot, then the fire key is held across the *next* `VIRTUAL_STEP_MS` (50ms) pump — so a shot resolves 0-50ms after the positions it was aimed at, while the target keeps moving.
  - **What proves it is motion and not aim: the effect is range-independent.** Displacement and sprite half-width are both world quantities divided by the same `dist` when projected, so their ratio survives projection and reduces to `speedMultiplier / spriteScale` — **4.0** for an Edge Case, 1.0 normal, **0.67** Elite (`speedFor` scales only Edge Cases; an Elite chases at a normal's speed and merely projects 1.5x). Edge Cases duly sit at **48-51% in every single distance bucket**, flat, which an aim problem cannot produce. In absolute terms an Edge Case covers up to 97% of its own sprite half-width per window (mean ~49%) against 24%/12% for normal/Elite.
  - **The controlled comparison that separates the two.** Bucketing by `angularHalfWidth / fireAngleEps` normalises distance, sprite and profile away; within one band the hit rate *still* splits **Elite 86% / normal 75% / Edge Case 48%**, in the exact order `speedMultiplier / spriteScale` predicts. That residual is defect 2; the band-to-band trend is defect 1.
  - **Why defect 2 is the one to fix first**: Edge Cases take ~40% of all ranged shots, so this alone is the largest single contributor to the headline number — and unlike defect 1 it silently taints every damage-per-second figure the bot has ever reported, since it under-counts damage *most* against the fastest archetype.
  - **Rejected on the data, not on inspection.** Overkill (a burst's later shots landing on an already-dead low-HP target) was the obvious confounder for the Edge Case number; splitting on whether the target still had more HP than one pistol round could remove shows **45-51% either way**, and normals show no overkill effect at all (94% vs 95%). It is not overkill.
  - **What this cannot see**: the engine's own cone deviation grows as `(range/FOG_FAR)³`, so the far-range rows blend the bot's error with the engine's spread — which is why the range breakdown is kept rather than pooled. `min(1, ratio)` in table 2 is the gate's *permission*, not a fit: the low-ratio rows beat it, because the bot aims better than its own gate requires.

- [ ] **Regular and Edge Case enemies do 75-100% of the damage and have no backlog item (2026-08-06).** Written in place of the refuted "level size is the real outlier" bullet. Across the 7-repo sweep, pooling raw `damageTaken.by`: Elites are **at most a quarter** of all damage a run takes (wolf3d 24.8%, codeenstein3d 21.3%, laravel 10.4%, curl 10.0%, ripgrep and the two zero-Elite repos 0%), while regular + Edge Case do the rest. Elites dominate only the *final* level (62-98%) — they are the full stop, not the attrition.
  - **ripgrep is the pure case**: 0% Elite damage, its single Elite never reached, and it still lost every one of 45 runs. An Elite clamp would do nothing for it.
  - **Deliberately not a proposed fix.** This is a hypothesis about where to look, not a measured problem with a known size — and per `remediation-order-2026-08-06.md` it should not be sized until the bot fixes land, since the current numbers are contaminated by the bot walking 14,000 tiles for loot and knife-trading Elites.
  - **What would size it**: after Stage C's re-measure, the share of deaths where no Elite was present at all, and the damage-per-enemy the attrition archetypes actually deliver. Review §10.

- [ ] **`verify:campaign`'s TODO-outcome coverage is ~8% fragile to any rng-shifting change (found 2026-08-10).** Each TODO comment rolls one of three outcomes (trap / mine / Bug enemy) with equal probability — `placeTodoEncounter` is a single `rng()` split into thirds — and the demo campaign carries 9 of them. So P(some outcome missing) is about 8% for *any* change that moves the shared rng stream, and the coloured-keys change landed there: the trap outcome vanished deterministically until a 10th TODO was added to `stage09_worker.go`. The check is worth keeping (it catches a showcase campaign that stopped demonstrating a mechanic) but it will keep biting. Options: more TODOs in the campaign, or make `placeTodoEncounter` cycle outcomes rather than roll them, which guarantees all three whenever a level has three — a small design change that trades randomness for a showcase guarantee.


- [ ] **Bot wedges — CORRECTED 2026-08-10: both root causes this item chased are FIXED, and its headline numbers are pre-fix. What is left is a re-measure, not a fix.** Read this before quoting "30% of runs" at anything.
  - **The loot treadmill is fixed** (`44b01c0`, 2026-08-06). All four "candidate fixes" this item used to list as open shipped together: the last hop targets the drop's own coordinate (the engine collects within 0.5 of the drop, not of its tile centre, and centre-to-corner is 0.707); dynamic drops get an abandoned set and attempt counter keyed by coordinate, so an uncollectable one is written off instead of re-picked forever; the target is committed to while it is still on offer, instead of re-chosen on every waypoint; and a per-level `LOOT_BUDGET_FRACTION` budget bounds the total, since kills create drops and the pool otherwise refills faster than it drains. **Serilog level 9: 88,791 decisions / 14,268 tiles → 2,823 / 535**, loot 87% → 35%.
  - **Its successor is fixed too** (2026-08-06, see `history.md`). Fixing the treadmill let the campaign reach level 14, which wedged differently — "286 legs planned, `driveLegs` returns zero decisions". That was a **C# parser bug**, not navigation: an unmodified interface method defaulted to `private`, so 136 methods became 140 lockable rooms — **640 doors, 321 keys** — and `planRoute` gave up at `MAX_ITERATIONS`, returning `{ok:false, legs:286}` with legs populated even on failure. Levels that fail to plan went **1 → 0** across all 105 staged levels.
  - **So the numbers in the old headline — 121 of 405 runs (30%), serilog 60/60, two repos at 0% completion — are all pre-fix and have never been re-measured.** They describe the world of 2026-08-06. Quoting them today is quoting a fixed bug. On 2026-08-10 they steered a "what next" decision straight into work that was already done; the item is rewritten so that cannot happen twice.
  - **The one thing genuinely open here is the re-measure**, and it is a campaign, not an afternoon: what is the wedge rate now, across serilog/ripgrep/curl at minimum, against the pre-fix 121/405? Until that runs, "the wedge is fixed" is supported by one level on one repo plus a plan/engine gate, which is real evidence but not a rate.
  - **Acceptance, unchanged**: serilog completes at a rate comparable to sinatra's 88%, and curl runs without excluding `.sh`.
  - **NOT curl.** curl's 58/58 level-1 wedge is a *different bug* and still open — the browser and the route planner disagree about which file is level 1, so the bot drove a route for a map it was not standing on. It has its own entry below; do not count it toward this one.
  - **Level 17 is NOT a problem** — measured 2026-08-07; the alarming reading was a god-mode artifact. God mode removes the death that terminates an attempt, so a >30-minute "unbounded grind" was really "this bot never dies". With a real loadout level 17 is reached by 7 of 18 runs, all 7 die there, median 89s — 11% of a run that gets that far, well behind levels 6, 10 and 11. **General lesson**: a god-mode diagnostic measures the bot's navigation, never its cost, because the thing that bounds a real attempt is death.
  - Original 2026-08-02 investigation follows; its evidence still stands, only its priority was wrong. The one metric the layout rework made worse, and it survives normalisation: oscillation **4.4 -> 6.6 ticks/1k decisions**. Localised, so the next attempt starts with evidence rather than a hunch:
  - **Not the corridor vocabulary.** Feature-sited oscillations went *down* (5 -> 4). 13 of 22 are inside rooms, which the rework never touched — same count, same sizes, total room floor identical (4653 -> 4658 tiles). What did change is that rooms went from 65% to 81% of walkable space, because corridor floor halved.
  - **15 of 22 are circling, not ping-pong**: `signFlips` ~0-3 of ~70 ticks, ~15 tiles travelled for ~1.8 tiles of net displacement, repeating at *identical coordinates* across every attempt. The remaining ~7 are true ping-pong (`signFlips` ~90%).
  - Two confirmed sites: an open stretch of an 18x18 labyrinth room with no enemies or props anywhere near (`stage02_bootstrap.sh` ~(37,31)), and a one-tile corridor with an **armed spike trap in the adjacent tile** (`stage07_service.rb` ~(21,33)) — where `detectOscillation`'s own `allWaitingOnSpike` exclusion evidently isn't catching it. Worth checking that exclusion before touching navigation at all.
  - **Deliberately not fixed here.** Both look like bot navigation tuning (turn rate vs walk speed vs arrival epsilon), and `balancing-telemetry.md` requires the 3-combo A/B protocol at n=12 for a bot-behaviour change — an n=1 reading cannot carry a directional claim, which that doc records having learned the hard way. Related: the "decouple bot decisions from engine ... should also help with aim oszilattion" backlog item below.

- [ ] **Firefox may be un-skippable in CI after all — one untried pref (`media.peerconnection.ice.loopback`).** `verify-multiplayer-connect.mjs` is skipped for the `firefox` leg of the `verify-browser` matrix (`if: matrix.browser != 'firefox'`) because Firefox gathers **zero** ICE candidates on GitHub Actions: it finds its default-route interface by `connect()`-ing a UDP socket toward a public IP purely to ask the OS which interface would be used, and the runner has no internet-routable default route, so gathering yields nothing (Mozilla bug 1659672, closed RESOLVED INVALID). That diagnosis is solid; the conclusion "therefore unfixable" is the part that may not follow. The script's own comment lists what was ruled out — `obfuscate_host_addresses` (kept, different quirk), fake `getUserMedia` (kept, wrong restriction), `network.dns.disableIPv6` (no effect), `force_interface` (needs the runner's interface name, not discoverable) — and **`media.peerconnection.ice.loopback` is not among them.** That pref lets Firefox gather candidates on the loopback interface, which is precisely what this case needs: both peers are two contexts in *one* Playwright process on *one* machine, so loopback candidates are sufficient and no routable interface is required at all. It is what Mozilla's own WebRTC tests use for this. **Cheap to test.** ~~This dev box is a good stand-in for the runner — outbound UDP is blocked here too~~ — **that premise is dead (2026-08-03): UDP egress works here and Chromium multiplayer passes locally, so this box is *not* a stand-in for the runner's no-routable-default-route shape.** A local Firefox pass would therefore be weaker evidence than the original note assumed, and a local Firefox *failure* would be strong evidence (it would mean the pref doesn't help even with working egress). Still worth the ten minutes, just read the result the right way round. Test: add the pref to `FIREFOX_LAUNCH_OPTIONS`, run `CODEENSTEIN_VERIFY_BROWSER=firefox npm run verify:multiplayer-connect`, check `installIceDiagnostics()` reports candidates. If it works: drop the matrix skip and correct `doc/dev/testing.md` again. Caveats: untested, and Firefox pref names/behaviour drift between versions

- [ ] **Walking-distance audit — site (1) is FIXED, site (2) is still open (re-verified 2026-08-10).** Prompted by the Stage 6 constraint; the sweep found the problem is *narrow*, not general.
  - **FIXED: `driveToExit`'s blocker choice.** It used to sort exit-room enemies by `Math.hypot` and hunt the nearest, so an enemy 5 tiles away through a wall (20 by corridor) was hunted before one 8 tiles down an open corridor. It now ranks via `rankExitBlockers`, gated on `BOT_WALKING_DISTANCE_BLOCKERS`, which **defaults to true** (`combatPolicy.mjs`) — the `hypot` branch survives only as an A/B arm reachable through `CODEENSTEIN_TELEMETRY_TUNING`.
  - **STILL OPEN: `pickThreat` (`combatPolicy.mjs:~891`).** It computes `dist` as `Math.hypot` and both *gates* on it (`.filter(e => e.dist < profile.engageRadius)`) and ranks on it. An enemy 7 tiles away through a wall but 25 by corridor still reads as "close". This is the Stage 6 issue below, and the two should be done together.
  - **Correct as-is, deliberately:** mine blast radius / disarm range and `visibleMineNear` (blast geometry is euclidean), rocket `CLUSTER_RADIUS`, `ARRIVE_EPS`, `LEG_REPLAN_DRIFT_TILES`, `TELEPORT_JUMP_DETECT_TILES`, distance to the current waypoint (~1 tile, in LOS), and the anomaly detectors' path/net measurements — all physical, not travel.
  - **Loot detours already do the right thing:** the `hypot` prefilter in `maybeDetourForLoot` is conservative-safe (straight-line <= walking distance, so it can only reject genuinely-too-far items), after which it ranks on a real `bfsPath` length and re-checks the cap against that.
- [ ] **Stage 6 (pre-emptive engagement) — build it against WALKING distance, not straight-line.** `pickThreat` filters `e.alive && e.aggroed`, so the bot walks past enemies it can plainly see; Stage 6 relaxes that. **User constraint (2026-07-31), and it is the crux of the stage:** *only engage enemies that are a realistic threat — do not run a long corridor for an enemy that has no chance of aggroing the player.* Note `pickThreat` today ranks and gates on `Math.hypot` straight-line distance, so an enemy 7 tiles away through a wall but 25 tiles by corridor currently reads as "close". Relaxing the aggro filter on top of a straight-line gate would send the bot on exactly the detours the user is describing. The plan's own gate (LOS required, `dist <= AGGRO_RADIUS` 7.5 rather than `engageRadius` 9.5, forward hemisphere, profile-gated) is necessary but not sufficient — LOS plus a short straight line still admits an enemy across an unreachable gap. **The right measure exists already:** `PathField.distAt(x,y)` is a live BFS distance from the player's tile through the *current* grid (`src/engine/pathField.ts`), which is exactly "how far would I actually have to walk to reach it". If the PathField/grid-exposure work lands first, Stage 6 should gate on that rather than on `hypot`. Rollback thresholds for the stage are already tightened in the plan (`combatVsExplorationRatio` up >20%, `peakSimultaneousAggroed.max` up >=2, any rise in `desperation.timeAtZeroRangedAmmoSec`) and it wants `ATTEMPT_CAP=40` from the start, since its sign is unknown a priori and aggro is sticky and irreversible

- [ ] **Bot mine retreat now backpedals instead of turning around — logic verified, real-world effect UNMEASURED (2026-07-31).** `mineRetreat` used to spin to face away from the mine while holding `KeyW`, so a retreat that began facing the mine (the normal case — you walk into one before noticing) *advanced on it* while turning: captured on `stage03_legacy_api.php` moving 7.86 -> 7.41, i.e. 0.45 tiles closer, mine 2.5 -> 2.2, over five decisions. That wasted motion is what made the retreat unable to finish: `findDangerousMine` fires at `MINE_BLAST_RADIUS + reactionBuffer` (2.4 + 0.32 = **2.72**) and `findDisarmableMine` only starts *above* it, but the bot never got past **2.6** before burning all `MINE_TARGET_GIVEUP_TICKS` (40 — exactly the count observed) and abandoning the mine. So it could neither pass nor shoot it. **The fix is the user's observation, not the original one:** the bot never needed to turn at all. `moveForward` takes a signed step (`forwardSign -= 1` for `KeyS`, engine.ts) so reversing is the same speed as advancing, sprint included — and backing up keeps the bot *facing* the mine, so the disarm shot is available the instant it clears the blast radius rather than requiring a second 180. **The bot had never dispatched `KeyS` anywhere in its history**; the whole backward axis was unused (it is Stage 7 of the rework plan, never built). `KeyS` also added to `TRANSLATING_KEYS` so a wedged backpedal is visible to `detectHeldKeyNoMovement`. **Caveat, stated plainly: unit-tested but never observed firing in play.** Across ~118,000 logged decisions in four runs (8 levels x 4, 3 levels x 3, 3 levels x 8), `mineRetreat` fired **zero** times, so `KeyS` never appeared. The original capture had 40 `mineRetreat` rows, so the branch does fire sometimes — the encounter is simply much rarer than that one capture suggested. Two earlier "it's fixed" readings in this session were invalid for exactly that reason: the post-fix runs had zero `mineRetreat` rows, so 0 oscillation findings measured the encounter not happening, not the fix working. **Do not A/B this until it has been seen firing** — the event is likely too rare to move any aggregate metric

